title The gate written three times and read once -- every launcher admitting pond now reaches the master seal
prompt The HUSH seat prompt: roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, rota row N mod 5 advancing by hand past a repeat, QA on touch, read scope, ListAgents before a file moves; pick up the running thread and take the next Pond lap under the accrete-only gate boundary.
think THE PEER, NAMED BEFORE A FILE MOVED. ListAgents answered no reachable agents, so ps carried the naming again. Dream's codex loop (pid 1906938) and its hot roster child (pid 792259) both read /home/keeper/grain-dream; mine read /home/keeper/grain. Two seats, two checkouts, all lap -- the one-tree-per-star arrangement my last lap found had closed itself, holding for a second lap running.
think ROSTER COLD, AND IT COST THREE STARTS TO LEARN ITS OWN LENGTH. The runner takes about 35 minutes on this pier and my first two opens were wrapped in timeout 590 and timeout 900, both of which killed it mid-run -- 32 and 35 guards of ninety-odd, no tree_at_close, no run_verdict. A partial roster is not a cold open, so I ran it detached and held the tree still until it closed. The wait is the instrument's own cost and it belongs in the lap rather than around it.
think ROTA N=3723 by git rev-list --count HEAD, row 3 -- WATER, which the 09:42 lap already deep-read after hand-advancing off fire. A repeat, so I advanced by hand again to row 4: EARTH, Mercury, the concrete. Its threshold page teaches one method -- read the fact at the door, before interpretation. A stamp off the filename, a room token off the status line, a mark off the value's face. That method chose this lap's whole shape.
think REDS FIRST. Nine rows stand OPEN and seven are gated or another body's. %334's agent-closable half closed on the 09:42 lap. Nothing in my lane was closable, so I took the next constructive door my own prior recommend named: the Zed launch path, tools/l/launch-zed.sh.example, which carries an ENCLOSURE=pond branch no guard reads.
think WHAT THE DOOR ACTUALLY SAID. Reading for the branch found it is not one launcher but THREE. tools/ag/agent-jail.sh at lines 241-250, tools/cu/cursor-jail.sh at 119-131, and tools/l/launch-zed.sh.example at 38-46 each read an ENCLOSURE selector and admit the value pond only after bash tools/p/pond_exit_bron_master_seal.sh --require returns zero. That seal is the custody boundary for the season flip: it demands a detached signature verifying against the cold master fingerprint 06462132D3E63B834F976E03A81D720B9235FA7A in a keyring holding only the master's public form, so an agent inside the jail cannot open the season by writing a sentinel. tools/fixtures/p/pond_policy_launcher_scan.sh reads exactly one of the three, because that is the one Pond's record describes.
think A RULE WRITTEN THREE TIMES IS A RULE THREE FILES MAY QUIETLY COME TO DISAGREE ABOUT, and the disagreement had already begun where nobody was looking: agent-jail.sh:243 and cursor-jail.sh:123 each assign EXIT_BRON to a path the seal script sets for itself and neither reads it again, while launch-zed.sh.example assigns nothing. Two dead assignments and one absence -- harmless in themselves, and the visible sign that three copies are maintained apart.
think THE READING THE EARTH ROW FOUND. tools/p/pond_exit_bron_master_seal.sh line 15 carries a claim about its own callers -- "Shared by tools/cu/cursor-jail.sh - tools/l/launch-zed.sh - scorecard witness." It names two of three, and the missing one is tools/ag/agent-jail.sh, the launcher this pier's standing loop actually runs (the ITINERARY names ./tools/ag/agent-jail.sh in its live-state line). A door that says who comes through it, and the most frequent visitor was not on the list.
think DISCOVERY RATHER THAN A ROSTER, because %301 and %326 booked that same root twice: a meter recognizing its subjects by a list of names never reports the one nobody thought to add. So the scan asks git for every tracked file carrying the selector, and a fourth launcher written tomorrow is measured on the lap it arrives.
think THE PATTERN HAD TO BE ANCHORED ON THE RIGHT. ENCLOSURE_CONF and ENCLOSURE_SECRETS are different variables that five files read, and a loose pattern counts all five as selector sites. %175 booked exactly this shape one room over, where a dated-stamp pattern matched a stamp inside a longer filename. The anchor is ENCLOSURE([^A-Z0-9_]|$), and the control proves it by planting a file that reads only ENCLOSURE_CONF and asserting the site count does not move.
think THE SECOND GATE CAME FROM %311. A launcher could call the seal and never read its answer -- bash ... --require || true is a gate in shape and an open door in fact, the same vacuity as a needle the harness itself supplies. So the scan reads the seal call's POSITION, accepting three refusing spellings by name and reddening on a fourth, so a new spelling reaches a hand rather than passing on a resemblance.
think A THIRD READING FROM THE WEAKER MODE. --policy only proves the master pubkey is on disk and gpg is on PATH; --require is the one that demands VALIDSIG. A gate calling --policy would look exactly like a gate and check nothing that matters, so the scan requires --require on the seal line and names the weaker mode in its detail when it finds one.
obs MY OWN INSTRUMENT WAS BLIND IN THE DIRECTION IT WARNS ABOUT, and its staging run caught it. Pointed at a plain directory rather than a repository, git grep failed, my || true swallowed the error, and the scan printed verdict=green off zero candidates. I had already written the sentence one screen above -- a scan reporting zero because it found nothing is the failure this whole family exists to refuse -- and left the same hole one line under it. git is the discovery instrument, so a root git cannot read now reports verdict=unreadable, and the control proves it from both sides by removing a pen's .git and putting it back. This is the third firing of that family in this tree: %301 rooms discovered by dated basenames, %326 paths recognized by a roster of 17 names, and now a scan whose whole candidate set can be empty for a reason that is not a fact about the tree.
obs THE CONTROL READ ITSELF AS A LAUNCHER, which is honest and had to be answered carefully. Its heredocs plant ungated launchers, so the scan counted the control as a defect. Skipping tools/fixtures/ whole would blind the guard to a launcher filed there; skipping any *_control.sh would let a launcher escape by its own name. So the exemption is TWO conditions -- in the fixtures room AND named as a scan or control -- the same discipline the QA report card seats for its index reading, and the control proves both halves by planting identical bytes at tools/fixtures/x/plant_control.sh (skipped) and tools/l/plant_control.sh (bitten).
obs A REAL GIT REPOSITORY IN EVERY PEN, rather than a directory. The scan discovers with git grep on purpose, so a pen that is not a repository would make the guard read differently in its proof than on the tree, and a guard proven under a different reading than the one it ships is proven of nothing. Each pen is git init plus git add; no commit is taken, since git ls-files and git grep read the index.
obs FORTY-TWO BEHAVIORS, ZERO FAILURES -- WHICH IS THE READING THAT CANNOT TELL A WORKING CONTROL FROM A BLIND ONE. So I removed each mechanism in turn and watched the cases fall: --require requirement 2, refusing position 2, header naming 4, guard-instrument skip 2, git-repository check 2, case-statement admission 1, boundary anchor 1, comment stripping 3. Eight probes, eight bit, seventeen distinct case firings, and cmp confirmed a byte-identical restore after every one.
obs ONE ASSERTION WAS A TAUTOLOGY AND I CAUGHT IT LATE. A control case read sealed_gates off a scan run and then asserted the scan reported that number -- true always, proving nothing. It reads a literal 3 now, and 3 is the control's OWN construction, since new_pen copies exactly three launchers into the pen, so that number can never go stale against a tree it does not measure.
obs THE REPAIR IS ONE COMMENT AND IT MAKES A FALSE CLAIM TRUE. The seal's header now names all three callers, and the scan holds unnamed_callers at zero so it stays true. This touches no markdown and flips no selector: the ACCRETE-ONLY GATE BOUNDARY stands, the ENCLOSURE selector stays ai-jail, and every document that instructs or references ai-jail is unchanged. A guard that adds a reading is the accrete-only shape of this lane.
obs WHAT THE LIVE TREE READS. selector_sites=4 (three launchers and tools/e/enclosure.conf.example, which assigns the selector and admits nothing), pond_admitting=3, sealed_gates=3, ungated_pond=0, unrefused_seal=0, unnamed_callers=0 after the repair and 1 before it.
obs THE PER-HOST CONFIG IS OUT OF REACH AND THAT IS RIGHT. tools/e/enclosure.conf is gitignored, so git never offers it. A config SETS the selector and a launcher ADMITS it, and the gate lives entirely on the admitting side, so a host writing ENCLOSURE=pond still meets the seal in whichever launcher it starts.
obs TWO LAUNCHERS READ THE CONF AND CARRY NO SELECTOR, and neither is a custody hole. tools/cu/cursor-jail-macos.sh runs sandbox-exec rather than ai-jail and is macOS's own enclosure; tools/l/lane_kvm.sh execs a command directly. Ignoring ENCLOSURE=pond means launching the SAFE side, so it is a clarity gap rather than an escape, and both sit in another body's room. tools/l/launch-cursor.rish delegates to cursor-jail.sh and inherits the gate, which is why the scan correctly does not count it.
obs DECLINED TO BOOK A REDS ROW, for the third lap running and the same measured reason: construction/REDS.md holds 24,571 of its 24,576 bytes with zero foldable rows and %338 booked and Keaton's. The lesson is written into the scan's own header where the next reader of the instrument meets it.
obs ROSTER, COLD AND HOT. Cold: 92 guards, 90 green, 2 red, tree_at_open and tree_at_close both 5c1852fe68fb, tree_moved=no, over a 47-minute run with Dream working its own checkout throughout. The two reds are rule_twin at 39 over 36, which is custody gate %7 and Keaton's by name, and witness_reach, which is Mind's and is named above. Three earlier opens died to a timeout wrapper before reaching tree_at_close, which taught me the runner's real length rather than anything about the tree.
obs QA on touch: four artifacts graded at meter -- the scan, the control, the witness and this card -- each composite 94, letter A, truth 100 with every cited path resolving. Service judged 75 rather than handed a number, since named_by_card reads the guard's name and not its paths, and that is a quarter of the reading. No frame pushed.
obs A RED I FOUND AND CANNOT CLOSE FROM THIS BENCH, SURFACED RATHER THAN ROUTED AROUND. witness_reach reads RED on this lap's cold roster, one over on both of its ceilings: unreached=1109 against 1108, wholly_unreached=221 against 220. The attribution is measured rather than reasoned. My own 09:42 roster read 92 guards, 91 green, one red, so witness_reach was green then. Exactly three commits have landed since, and exactly one ADDED a witness -- 37200d28a8 at 09:57, LOCA, adding tools/m/ios_app_shell_witness.rish with no roster row. Neither the roster nor the scan's ceiling was touched by the other two.
obs WHY I LEFT IT FOR MIND. tools/m/ios_app_shell_witness.rish line 27 ASSERTS a booted simulator -- xcrun simctl list devices booted -- rather than gating off-platform, so putting it on the shared clock would red the roster on this Framework laptop and on every other non-macOS body. Teaching it to gate is a change to Mind's own witness in Mind's own lane, and a planet defers to its star where lanes brush. So it is named here with its commit, its stamp and its one line, which is what reds-first asks of a red a body cannot close.
obs THE REPAIR I COULD HAVE MADE AND DECLINED, NAMED SO NOBODY MAKES IT LATER. Two other witnesses stand dark and unrostered -- tools/am/amphora_first_resident_witness.rish (02:25) and tools/m/macos_app_bundle_witness.rish (01:08) -- and both were already inside the 1108 that read green at 09:42. Rostering EITHER of them would carry unreached back to 1108 and turn the guard green while the actually-new dark witness stayed dark. The guard's own message says put THE NEW ONE on a clock rather than raising the ceiling, and rostering a different one is that same evasion through a side door. A ratchet satisfied by an unrelated repair has stopped measuring what it was written to measure.
obs THE FETCH CORRECTED TWO OF MY OWN SENTENCES ABOVE, AND I LEFT THEM STANDING BESIDE THE CORRECTION. `git fetch xy` before packaging brought four commits, and one of them is the Mac bench answering this same red two and a half hours before I wrote about it -- 5646a0dfa5, `construction: clock the Mac bundle guard`, log 20260829.104233. First: I wrote that rostering the iOS witness would red every Linux body, and REDS %295 already seated a `host` field for exactly that -- a row carrying `host macos` is skipped BY NAME on Linux, printed as SKIPHOST, so a host-scoped row would not have reddened anything here. Second: I wrote that rostering a different dark witness to bring the count under the ceiling would be an evasion, and the Mac bench rostered `macos_app_bundle` at `tier cadence host macos` after running it GREEN there in 4s. That is not the evasion I described; it is the debt being paid by the only bench that could pay it. My reasoning was right for THIS bench, where I can prove neither witness green, and wrong as a general claim.
obs AND THE MAC BENCH'S RULING ON THE iOS WITNESS IS SHARPER THAN MINE. Its card line reads `ios` declines a clock -- a booted simulator is state. That is the real reason, and it outranks my platform argument: a roster run cannot assume a simulator is booted, so the witness is unclockable rather than merely off-platform. Its own ceiling comment says the rest plainly -- the fall the macOS repair earned is exactly spent by the iOS birth, so the ceiling STANDS at 1108 rather than falling, said out loud rather than absorbed.
obs WHAT THE MERGE ALSO HANDED ME IS MY OWN NEXT DOOR. The Mac bench's card reads `Roster:` 4 red of 92, `rule_twin` is gate %7, and THREE POND ROWS ARE HUSH'S. All three read green on this pier and red there, which fits a host boundary rather than a fault -- Pond's enclosure guards drive ai-jail, and a Mac has none. The hypothesis is that they want `host linux` rows the way the Mac bundle guard wanted `host macos`, and I am naming it as a hypothesis because I cannot measure a macOS red from this bench. It is the next door on the card.
obs MY OWN WITNESS LANDS WITH ITS ROSTER ROW IN THE SAME COMMIT, which is the discipline this red exists to teach. pond_seal_gate goes on the lap tier beside pond_enclosure_door, and the scan re-read after the landing confirms it joins `standing` rather than `unreached`.
